.cs 1 on
TBROWSE (LOCAL CMS COMMAND)
.sp  1
TBROWSE is used to scan tapes in a full-screen BROWSE-like environment.
.sp 2
.cs 1 off
.cs 2 on
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  TBROWSE    <maxblock< cuu>>
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
.tr ¬ 40
.of 11
.fo off
Defaults - maxblock - 4240
cuu¬¬¬¬¬¬¬- 181
.fo on
.of
.sp 2
.cs 2 off
.cs 3 on
.of 9
maxblock¬is the largest block this program should encounter in the
current file. Upper limit is memory size (up to 16m)
.sp 1
.of 9
cuu¬¬¬¬¬¬is the virtual address of the tape to be scanned (181-184).
.of
.sp 2
TBROWSE SUBCOMMANDS:
.sp 1
Several subcommands are available to help you TBROWSE a tape.
Valid subcommands are:
.sp 2
QUIT SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  QUIT
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the QUIT subcommand to exit immediately from TBROWSE leaving the
tape positioned
following the last block viewed. The QUIT subcommand closes any printer
spool file opened by the PRINT subcommand.
.sp 2
CP,CMS SUBCOMMANDS
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  CP
  CMS
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the CP, CMS subcommands to enter respectively into CP
(return with 'B') or CMS
(return with 'RETURN').
.sp 2
PRINT SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  PRINT
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the PRINT subcommand to print the current screen of data
on the
virtual printer (00E).
'*** END OF BLOCK ***' is printed highlighted as on screen.
Note that the printer spool file is only closed when the QUIT subcommand
is executed. If TBROWSE is terminated prematurely, issue the CMS "finis"
command to close the file.
.sp 2
DISK SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  DISK       < n < fn < ft < fm >>>>
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the DISK subcommand to copy blocks, starting with the one currently
displayed, on to the CMS file "fn ft fm". If not specified the default
fileid is "TBROWSE CMSUT1 A1"  Files are copied using the current mode
and ASCII translation settings. If the mode becomes invalid or an error
is encountered, the DISK function will terminate
and TBROWSE will display the block that couldn't be written.
.sp 1
.of 9
n¬¬¬¬¬¬¬¬specifies how many blocks to dump. The default is one.
.of
.sp 2
SAVE SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  SAVE       < fn < ft < fm >>>
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
The SAVE subcommand behaves like the DISK command except that all blocks
are copied from the one currently displayed until the end-of-file.
.sp 2
V SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  V          <n>
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the V subcommand to set the first column displayed.
The setting applies to the block currently
displayed and all following blocks. The L and R subcommands give
similar capability.
.sp 1
.of 9
n¬¬¬¬¬¬¬¬is the starting column. The default is one.
.of
.sp 2
MODE SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  MODE       <F <n>|V|VB|U>
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the MODE subcommand to set the formatting mode of the blocks read
starting with the one currently displayed.
.sp 1
.of 9
F¬¬¬¬¬¬¬¬will set fixed (blocked) mode.
.sp 1
.in 9
.of 9
n¬¬¬¬¬¬¬¬is the LRECL to be used in F mode. The default is 80.
.in
.sp 1
.of 9
V¬¬¬¬¬¬¬¬will set variable mode. The length of each block will
be taken from information in the record.
.sp 1
.of 9
VB¬¬¬¬¬¬¬will set variable blocked mode. The logical record length
and the block length will be taken from information in the record.
.sp 1
.of 9
U¬¬¬¬¬¬¬¬will set undefined block mode. The physical block will be
treated as a single record.
This mode is chosen when ever another mode does not apply.
.of
.sp 1
.il 3
Modes U and V display the current record such that it
wraps around from line to line.
Modes F and VB, on the other hand, display one logical record per line
with truncation occuring if the record plus the display
column is greater then the console line width.
.sp 2
FSR SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  FSR        <n>
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the FSR subcommand to advance the tape one or more blocks.
If end of file
is sensed, the last block of the file is displayed.
.sp 1
.of 9
n¬¬¬¬¬¬¬¬specifies how many blocks to skip. The default is one.
.of
.sp 2
BSR SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  BSR        <n>
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the BSR subcommand to move the tape back one or more blocks.
If end of file
is sensed, the first block of the file is displayed.
.sp 1
.of 9
n¬¬¬¬¬¬¬¬specifies how many blocks to move. The default is one.
.of
.sp 2
FSF SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  FSF        <n>
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the FSF subcommand to advance the tape to the next file
and display the first block of that file.
If end of tape
is sensed, the last file on the tape is displayed.
.sp 1
.of 9
n¬¬¬¬¬¬¬¬specifies how many files to skip. The default is one.
.of
.sp 2
BSF SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  BSF        <n>
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the BSF subcommand to move the tape to the previous file
and display the first block of that file.
If start of tape
is sensed, the first file on the tape is displayed.
.sp 1
.of 9
n¬¬¬¬¬¬¬¬specifies how many files to move. The default is one.
.of
.sp 2
REW SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  REW
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the REW subcommand to rewind the tape and display the first
block of the first file.
.sp 2
TOP SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  TOP
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the TOP subcommand to move the tape to the first block
in the current file.
.sp 2
BOT SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  BOT
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the BOT subcommand to move the tape to the last block
in the current file.
.sp 2
F, FH SUBCOMMANDS
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  F
  FH
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the F subcommand to advance within a block to the next
screenful of
records. If less than a screen of records remains, the
message '*** END OF BLOCK ***' is displayed following the
last record.
The FH subcommand is identical to the F subcommand except
that only half a screen of records is skipped.
.sp 2
B, BH SUBCOMMANDS
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  B
  BH
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the B subcommand to move within a block to the previous
screenful of
records. If less than a screen of records remains, the
message '*** END OF BLOCK ***' is displayed following the
last record.
The BH subcommand is identical to the B subcommand except
that only half a screen of records is skipped.
.sp 2
RESET SUBCOMMANDS
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  RESET
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the RESET subcommand to reset the display start column
and the top of screen record number to 1.
.sp 2
L SUBCOMMANDS
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  L          <n>
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the L subcommand to move the display column to the left.
.sp 1
.of 9
n¬¬¬¬¬¬¬¬is the number of columns to move. The default is the screen
width.
.of
.sp 2
R SUBCOMMANDS
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  R          <n>
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the R subcommand to move the display column to the right.
.sp 1
.of 9
n¬¬¬¬¬¬¬¬is the number of columns to move. The default is the screen
width.
.of
.sp 2
HEX SUBCOMMANDS
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  HEX        <ON|OFF>
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the HEX subcommand to set hexadecimal display mode.
.sp 1
.of 9
ON¬¬¬¬¬¬¬will causerecords/blocks
to be displayed in hexadecimal format.
.sp 1
.of 9
OFF¬¬¬¬¬¬will cause records/blocks to be displayed in
character format.
.sp 1
.of 9
nothing¬¬HEX with no parm will toggle the option.
.of
.sp 2
ASCII SUBCOMMANDS
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
  ASCII      <ON|OFF>
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Use the ASCII subcommand to alter the ASCII translation on input setting.
.sp 1
.of 9
ON¬¬¬¬¬¬¬will set the ASCII translate on input mode so
all records/blocks displayed will be translated from ASCII to EBCDIC.
.sp 1
.of 9
OFF¬¬¬¬¬¬will reset the ASCII translate on input mode so
all records/blocks displayed will not be translated from ASCII to EBCDIC.
.sp 1
.of 9
nothing¬¬ASCII with no parm will toggle the option.
.of
.sp 2
n SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
   n
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
Type a number as a subcommand to set the top of screen record.
.sp 2
= SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
   =
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
The = subcommand repeats the last command.  The last
command entered will be executed again.
.sp 2
? SUBCOMMAND
.sp 1
.fo off
.bx 1 12 40
   ?
.bx off
.fo on
.sp 1
The ? subcommand displays the last command entered.
The command will be executed only if the
user presses the ENTER key.
.sp 2
& PREFIX FUNCTION
.sp 1
The & character can be used as a prefix for TBROWSE subcommands to
leave the subcommand in the display area after execution; this allows
for repetitive execution.
.sp 2
.fo off
Most of the subcommands are accessible by PA/PF keys as follows:
.sp 1
.bx 11 21 31
.sp 1
            PA1  CP   PA2 CMS
.sp 1
.bx off
.sp 1
.bx 5 16 27 38
.sp 1
       1  BSR    2  FSR     3  QUIT
.sp 1
.bx
.sp 1
       4  L      5  R       6  RESET
.sp 1
.bx
.sp 1
       7  F      8  B       9  DISK
.sp 1
.bx
.sp 1
       10 BSF    11 FSF     12 PRINT
.sp 1
.bx off
.sp 1
NOTE: PF13..PF24 are mapped onto PF1..PF12
.fo on
.cs 3 off
